From 6bb32ea002498f8c77b2a25cd481ceae1fd6c692 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 03:34:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] generalized-bulletproofs: skip a proof's transcript
 without decoding its points

Why: skip_verify_transcript (used by Fcmp::verify to peek the second
proof's z past the first proof, on a clone) read every point with
read_point, which decodes it, only to move the transcript forward. The
first proof's verify then decodes the same points again. In a profile of
1-input FCMP++ blocks on a Ryzen 9 3900X these decodings were 0.6-2.7% of
the node's CPU.

VerifierTranscript gains skip_point (crate-private), which transcripts a
point's bytes exactly as read_point does and advances past them without
decoding. skip_verify_transcript uses it for every point. Scalars are still
read with read_scalar.

Safety:
- The transcript state after a skip is unchanged: read_point already
  transcripted the encoded bytes, not a re-encoding of the decoded point.
- A point that does not decode no longer fails the skip; it fails the
  first proof's verify, which reads every one of these points with
  read_point, on the real transcript. The proof is rejected either way.
- Fcmp::verify still checks that the real transcript after the first
  verify equals the skipped clone.
- Verification only; public data.

Tests: fuzz_test_arithmetic_circuit now also corrupts the first point the
skip passes (AI) and the last (the inner-product argument's last R) in each
generated proof: the skip succeeds and lands where it lands for the valid
proof, and verify rejects.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
